---
import CaseStudy from "../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro";
import KettleSitemapDiagram from "../../components/KettleSitemapDiagram.astro";

const facts = [
  { k: "Role", v: "Sole UX designer" },
  { k: "Team", v: "CTO, Head of Product, 3 engineers" },
  { k: "Year", v: "2021 · Contract" },
];

const sources = [
  {
    label: "Interaction Design for Trees",
    by: "Hagan Rivers",
    href: "https://medium.com/@hagan.rivers/interaction-design-for-trees-5e915b408ed2",
  },
  {
    label: "Designing an Admin Panel",
    by: "Christian Behler",
    href: "https://uxdesign.cc/designing-an-admin-panel-3e77c19870f4",
  },
  {
    label: "Apple Human Interface Guidelines",
    by: "Apple",
    href: "https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/",
  },
  {
    label: "Material Design",
    by: "Google",
    href: "https://m2.material.io/design/guidelines-overview",
  },
];

const compares = [
  {
    what: "Editing a user",
    before: [
      "Every field forced into one small modal",
      "Schedule and permitted locations collapsed together, hard to read",
    ],
    after: [
      "Full screen to work in",
      "Schedule and permitted locations separated and visible at a glance",
    ],
    shots: [
      {
        src: "/work/kettleos/edit-modal-before.gif",
        alt: "Before: editing a work group in a small modal stacked over the dimmed page, with Group Meta, Members, Locations & Schedule, and Health & Access crammed into one narrow panel.",
        width: 1434,
        height: 877,
        label: "Before",
      },
      {
        src: "/work/kettleos/edit-fullscreen-after.gif",
        alt: "After: editing a work group on a full screen with tabbed Details, Schedule, Permitted Locations, and Members sections.",
        width: 1439,
        height: 899,
        label: "After",
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    what: "Viewing a location",
    before: [
      "One page, a different display pattern for each kind of data",
      "Several “Manage” buttons",
      "Color combinations that failed WCAG (white on green, white on red)",
    ],
    after: [
      "Tabbed sections, all using the same pattern",
      "One global Edit button",
      "Every color combination AA or better",
    ],
    shots: [
      {
        src: "/work/kettleos/viewing-location-before-after.webp",
        alt: "Before and after of a location page. Before: a single crowded page mixing a photo carousel, a floor list, and a reservations table. After: a Finder-style sidebar of locations next to one location's Details, Schedule, and floor table.",
        width: 1747,
        height: 806,
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    what: "Deactivating and floors",
    before: [
      "A prominent Deactivate button sitting in the modal",
      "Floor information nested in its own set of modals",
    ],
    after: [
      "Deactivate moved behind the Edit screen, so it's a deliberate choice",
      "Floor information shown as a table",
    ],
    shots: [
      {
        src: "/work/kettleos/editing-location-before-after.webp",
        alt: "Before and after of editing a location. Before: a small Edit Location modal pulled out over the dimmed page. After: a full-screen Edit Location view with Details, Schedule, and Floors tabs.",
        width: 1909,
        height: 783,
      },
    ],
  },
];
---

<CaseStudy
  title="KettleOS admin panel · Cat Tran"
  description="Information architecture and a consistent pattern system for KettleOS, the desktop admin panel behind Kettle's hybrid-work platform."
  client="Kettle"
  heading="KettleOS admin panel"
  lede="KettleOS had grown into a tangle of one-off patterns and ambiguous Edit buttons. I replaced it with a small, reusable pattern system, two layouts doing the work of a dozen, built on established admin conventions rather than invented from scratch."
  facts={facts}
>
  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Context</h2>
    <p>
      Kettle is a hybrid-work platform for managing office real estate and
      scheduling. After a Scan &amp; Reserve pilot with NYU during the pandemic,
      Kettle wanted to fold space reservations into its product and grow the B2B
      relationship. Over eight weeks in 2021 I redesigned KettleOS, the desktop
      admin panel Kettle's ops teams use to manage every location, space, and
      access rule.
    </p>
    <p>
      I was the sole UX designer, working with the CTO, the Head of Product, and
      three engineers. We met at standup every day and shipped one small piece at
      a time.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Testers found the existing panel hard to learn. Varying colors, shapes, and
      hierarchies made it difficult to tell what was what. Editing was the worst
      of it: too many <strong>Edit</strong> buttons, each opening a separate
      modal, with no clear sense of what a given edit would change. The prototype
      also needed to move from Sketch and InVision into Figma. I had never
      designed an admin panel before.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Learning from what exists</h2>
    <p>
      Rather than invent, I spent the first days reading about admin-panel design
      and pulling apart existing products, so I could reuse proven conventions
      and spend my own time on what was actually specific to Kettle.
    </p>
    <ul>
      {
        sources.map((s) => (
          <li>
            <a href={s.href}>{s.label}</a> &nbsp;·&nbsp; {s.by}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Information architecture</h2>
    <p>
      I started by diagramming the data structure and the hierarchy of objects in
      Miro, then mapped the existing sitemap and proposed a new one. The core
      objects (Locations, Work Groups, Users) moved to the top level, and the
      edit structure was standardized so that <strong>add</strong> lives at the
      top level and <strong>edit</strong> lives at the individual level. After
      that, the path to view or edit a Location, a Work Group, or a User all
      looked the same.
    </p>

    <figure class="shot">
      <KettleSitemapDiagram />
      <figcaption>
        The proposed architecture pulls the core objects up a level and makes
        every edit path identical: one Add at the top of each section, one
        Edit beneath every item.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <p>
      For navigation I compared the usual tree patterns and chose a{" "}
      <strong>Finder pattern</strong>: right for a small, finite number of layers
      (unlike Miller Columns) holding non-tabular data (unlike a collapsible
      table).
    </p>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/work/kettleos/nav-pattern-comparison.webp"
        alt="Three navigation patterns compared: Finder, with a hierarchical sidebar and file list; a collapsible table with nested rows; and Miller Columns, with cascading panes. Finder is circled as the chosen pattern."
        width="2000"
        height="750"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        Finder vs. Miller Columns vs. collapsible table.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">A two-pattern system</h2>
    <p>
      The old panel used a different display pattern for almost every kind of
      data. I catalogued all of them and found the content really only came in
      two shapes: <strong>key / value pairs</strong> and <strong>tables</strong>.
      One simple pattern for each, used everywhere, did most of the work of
      making the panel feel consistent.
    </p>

    <ul class="stats">
      <li>
        <span class="stat-v">5+ → 2</span>
        <span class="stat-k">
          one-off display patterns collapsed into one for key/value data, one
          for tables
        </span>
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>On top of that:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>the scattered Edit buttons became a single global Edit</li>
      <li>
        editing moved out of cramped modals onto full screens with tabbed
        sections
      </li>
      <li>
        every color combination was brought to AA or better; the old panel had
        white-on-green and white-on-red that failed
      </li>
      <li>
        engineers pulled components from Grommet, a React framework, so I mapped
        my Figma components to theirs
      </li>
    </ul>

    <figure class="shot">
      <img
        src="/work/kettleos/two-pattern-system.webp"
        alt="A Work Group detail screen showing the key/value pattern (Name, Team Leader, Description as labeled fields) on its Details tab, and the table pattern (a Permitted Locations table and a Members table) on its other tabs."
        width="1909"
        height="1195"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>
        The two patterns the whole panel is built from: key/value pairs for
        object details, tables for lists.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Before and after</h2>
    {
      compares.map((c) => (
        <>
          <h3 class="compare-what">{c.what}</h3>
          <div class="compare">
            <div class="before">
              <p class="compare-label">Before</p>
              <ul>
                {c.before.map((b) => (
                  <li>{b}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
            <div class="after">
              <p class="compare-label">After</p>
              <ul>
                {c.after.map((a) => (
                  <li>{a}</li>
                ))}
              </ul>
            </div>
          </div>
          <figure class="shot">
            {c.shots.length > 1 ? (
              <div class="shot-stack">
                {c.shots.map((s) => (
                  <div>
                    {s.label && <p class="shot-stack-label">{s.label}</p>}
                    <img
                      src={s.src}
                      alt={s.alt}
                      width={s.width}
                      height={s.height}
                      loading="lazy"
                    />
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>
            ) : (
              <img
                src={c.shots[0].src}
                alt={c.shots[0].alt}
                width={c.shots[0].width}
                height={c.shots[0].height}
                loading="lazy"
              />
            )}
          </figure>
        </>
      ))
    }
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">Where it landed</h2>
    <p>
      KettleOS shipped into production on two patterns, one standardized edit
      path for every object type, an accessible color set, and into Figma with
      auto-layout so engineers could map directly to their Grommet components.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="eyebrow">What was hard</h2>
    <ul class="hard">
      <li>
        <strong>Recruiting testers.</strong> Pandemic-era membership was
        inactive, so I leaned on coworkers who worked with members every day,
        choosing only people with no hand in building the app. I interviewed them
        alongside the usability tests, and that extra context ended up shaping the
        final design.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Handoff.</strong> Engineers kept asking what components in my
        Figma file were called so they could find them in Grommet. I should have
        annotated components with their library names from the start.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Auto-layout.</strong> I picked it up on this project and now
        can't imagine designing without it.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>
</CaseStudy>

<style>
  .shot-stack {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 22px;
  }
  .shot-stack-label {
    font-size: 11px !important;
    letter-spacing: 0.13em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    color: var(--warm-grey);
    margin: 0 0 8px !important;
  }
</style>
